<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Biophys Biochem Cytol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0095-9901</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">13357562</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2229678</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>MITOCHONDRIA OF PROTOZOA</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sedar</surname><given-names>Albert W.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Rudzinska</surname><given-names>Maria A.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1bd72f0N0x330b148">From the Department of Anatomy, The Jefferson Medical College of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, and The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>25</day><month>7</month><year>1956</year></pub-date><volume>2</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>331</fpage><lpage>336</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1956, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> A study of thin sections of <italic>Paramecium multimicronucleatum, Tetrahymena pyriformis, Tokophrya infusionum</italic>, and <italic>Amoeba proteus</italic> shows that the mitochondria in all these protozoa are similar in certain aspects of their fine structure to that described in metazoan cells. As in higher organisms the mitochondrion is surrounded by a double limiting membrane and contains protrusions directed inward from the innermost of the double membranes. There are, however, some differences. In a majority of higher organisms the internal structure of mitochondria consists of ridges or cristae mitochondriales and in a few instances only of finger-like projections, or microvilli. In all protozoa described here and elsewhere microvilli represent the dominant structure. They are characteristic therefore of protozoan mitochondria. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>